dlbk5dump recovers family-history data from Brother's Keeper 5 databases.
Build with GNU make and a C++17 compiler, then run from the repository root
with the legacy files in BK5/. Database files are read-only inputs.

    make
    ./dlbk5dump --gedcom > indexed.ged
    ./dlbk5dump --gedcom --clean > indexed-clean.ged

GEDCOM generation is implemented entirely in C++; Python is not required.
The default audit export preserves extraction notes, source addresses, raw
date slots, ALTKEYS slots and unassigned BKMAIL address-book text. --clean
omits extraction notes and the address-book inventory while retaining
genealogical notes, citations, event places and unresolved information.
Both modes produce GEDCOM 5.5.1 with UTF-8 text and a submitter record.

The Makefile provides the same exports:

    make gedcom                 # audit GEDCOM to stdout
    make gedcom-file            # indexed.ged
    make gedcom-clean-file      # indexed-clean.ged

File targets generate a temporary file and replace the old export only on
success. When capturing make's stdout, use make -s gedcom > indexed.ged:
otherwise compiler commands can appear there if a rebuild is needed. The
direct ./dlbk5dump command always sends only GEDCOM to stdout; diagnostics
go to stderr. --clean is accepted only after --gedcom.

The Python exporter tools/bk5-indexed-to-gedcom.py is retained as a reference.
The native exporter uses the same C++ person, family and lookup decoders
in process, and implements the reference's formatting and ordering rules.
To compare every output byte, including clean mode:

    make check-gedcom

This requires Python 3 for the reference and checks the bundled database,
CP437 text, missing/malformed inputs, and native export with no executables
on PATH. Comparison outputs and synthetic inputs use temporary directories;
the existing indexed.ged and BK5 files are not modified by this check.
Before changing export behavior, save the previous GEDCOM for comparison.

Independent validation (Python 3 required):

    make audit
    ./tools/gedcom-check.py indexed.ged
    ./tools/gedcom-roles.py indexed.ged
    ./tools/gedcom-places.py indexed.ged
    ./tools/bk5-ancestry.py indexed.ged --person 3 --terminals

The export remains an intermediate extraction: raw-only records appear
under synthetic @IR...@ identifiers, and ambiguous dates or references are
retained as labelled notes. No missing person or relationship is invented.
The ancestry tool follows explicit FAMC links only.

Useful decoder commands:

    ./dlbk5dump --dump-altkeys
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-id-records
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-records
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-families
    ./dlbk5dump --trace-family 31
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-sources
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-locations
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-marriages
    ./dlbk5dump --family 0x9a
    ./tools/bk5-mail.py
    ./tools/bk5-options.py

--dump-altkeys replaces tools/bk5-altkeys.py for normal use. It needs only
BK5/ALTKEYS.DT5 and runs entirely in C++. It checks that the file consists
of complete 162-byte slots, decodes CP437 text before the first '<', and
reports non-empty slots with their original zero-based slot numbers. Blank
slots contribute to the summary count; slot numbers are not person IDs.
The GEDCOM exporter uses the same ALTKEYS reader. For this database:

    ALTKEYS_SLOT slot=16 text='Bournemouth, Dorset'
    ALTKEYS_SLOTS total=19 nonempty=1

The Python script remains an independent reference. Run make check-altkeys
to compare outputs byte for byte on the bundled file and temporary fixtures
covering every CP437 byte, control/quote escaping, whitespace, full and empty
slots, and invalid or missing input. The check also verifies that the native
flag works without other database files or executables on PATH.

The person decoder reads the key-0 index, then scans physical fixed slots
for valid person numbers missing from the index. Raw-page scans preserve a
second view of the variable data, including old or orphaned records.

Family membership comes from the 167 decoded BKMARR.DT5 family records,
including spouse and child IDs. Person references not confirmed by that
table remain notes. Marriage dates, places, source citations, ceremony
status and documented divorce/annulment/separation codes are exported.
--dump-marriages is an older diagnostic date scan; --dump-families is the
record-aware family decoder. --dump-relations reports unvalidated B-tree
pointer candidates and must not be used as genealogy relationships.

Person date/location slots are birth, baptism, death, burial and other.
Slots 1-4 produce matching events with dates and/or places; slot 5 and raw
dates of unproven type remain notes. BKSOURCE descriptions become standalone
SOUR records. Person citations, with PAGE and QUAY, attach to the matching
event when exported, otherwise to the person; family citations stay on the
family. All five person source/location slot notes remain in audit mode.

ALTKEYS and BKMAIL text stays unassigned unless ownership is independently
proven. BKOPTION and its backup are configuration, not genealogy data.
